WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.080
If you’ve ever wanted to have your&nbsp;
art exist outside your screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.760
this tutorial will help you achieve&nbsp;
that by going through some basics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.920
as well as more advanced techniques of exporting&nbsp;
your files for print in Clip Studio Paint!

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.440
Setting up Image Dimensions &amp; Resolution

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.240
The most basic things you want to keep&nbsp;
in mind when preparing a file for print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.840
are the image dimension &amp; resolution values.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.520
When you create a new file in Clip Studio&nbsp;
Paint, you are met with this dialogue window:

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.320
Since we intend to print things, let’s switch&nbsp;
over to the third button in the top row.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.680
This option is called “show all Comic settings”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.200
but even if you just want to make&nbsp;
an illustration rather than a comic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.320
the more advanced settings that are available&nbsp;
here will be helpful to optimize files for print!

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.720
Let’s go over all these options.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.800
First off, the “Unit” (1) option in the top&nbsp;
right corner. It makes sense to now either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.480
select the cm (centimeter), mm (millimeter) or&nbsp;
in (inch) option. When preparing files for print,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.840
pixels or points aren’t going to be super helpful.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.560
In the “Canvas” section we can decide Canvas&nbsp;
Width and Height (2), but these options are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.840
okay to ignore for now, as the more detailed&nbsp;
settings below will actually decide them for us!

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.880
The one thing we need to pay attention to is&nbsp;
the “Resolution” option (3). If you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.840
print something in good quality, you need to have&nbsp;
this set to 300 (dpi / dots per inch) at least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.040
600 will be even better depending on the&nbsp;
printing quality and technique. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.080
computer can handle a large file size well, I&nbsp;
would recommend going with the higher value.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.120
The “Basic expression color” option (4)&nbsp;
determines whether your image will be in color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.160
grayscale or monochrome. This tutorial&nbsp;
will only focus on the color option.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.680
The “Binding (finish) size” option (5) is going&nbsp;
to be the actual final size of your artwork.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.702
Clip Studio Paint already includes a few common&nbsp;
image size templates (currently selected:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.702 --> 00:02:14.960
“A4 size”), which you can pick from. These&nbsp;
will change the Canvas Width and Height values.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.400
Below these values, you’ll&nbsp;
find an important option called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.840
“Bleed width” (6). If you&nbsp;
are unfamiliar with the term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.440
it refers to extra artwork being printed&nbsp;
beyond the final artwork size. Do we need this?

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.800
Well, it depends. If you want to print something&nbsp;
like an art print that’s going to be framed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.000
will have a white border around it, you won’t&nbsp;
need it. If you want to print something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.880
a poster that’s gonna have your artwork&nbsp;
take up the entire paper, you will need it.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.040
The reason for that is that you won’t be&nbsp;
able to print on the very edge of a paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.680
So to have artwork extend all the way to the&nbsp;
edge, you actually need to print something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.280
in a larger format, and then crop it to the&nbsp;
smaller format. Now, if there is no bleed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.920
the cropping process might not be exact and result&nbsp;
in white edges on the paper. Bleed helps ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.200
that even if things aren’t cropped precisely,&nbsp;
we will have artwork perfectly fill out a paper.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.440
What should we set “Bleed width” to? You&nbsp;
will need to check with your printer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.360
Different services have different&nbsp;
standards. Some say 2mm, some may say 5mm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.600
It can also depend on what you are printing or in&nbsp;
what size. So just head to your printing service’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.600
website and look for that information&nbsp;
in the print specifications section.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.440
The “Default border (inner) size” option (7) is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.680
bit less relevant if you are creating an&nbsp;
illustration. If you are creating comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.320
this would usually be used to determine the&nbsp;
area of the page where panels are visible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.680
with only page spreads or certain artwork that&nbsp;
extends beyond panels going beyond that area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.000
You can switch between determining the border size&nbsp;
(“set size”) or the border itself (“set margin”).

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.960
I won’t go into the “Safety&nbsp;
margin” option (8) here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.800
as its use is explained well by the&nbsp;
official Clip Studio Paint article.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.680
https://tips.clip-studio.com/en-us/articles/1858&nbsp;
In my example, we won’t be using it.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.320
We can now press “OK” to create the file.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.400
Changing Image Dimensions Retroactively

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.000
Some of you might have already&nbsp;
created artwork without setting your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.520
file up as described in the&nbsp;
previous section. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.640
if you want to prepare your file for print&nbsp;
now, you can still access these options.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.160
If you want to check that your image resolution&nbsp;
meets the 300 dpi quality requirement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.960
go to “Edit” &gt; “Change image resolution”.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.880
At the bottom, you can see the&nbsp;
“Unit” and “Resolution” options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:59.120
If you set your file up in a pixel format,&nbsp;
it should still show as pixels, at 72 dpi.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.120
First, select 300 dpi in the “Resolution” menu&nbsp;
(or whatever other option you want to aim for).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.600
You will now see the “Width”,&nbsp;
“Height” and “Scale“ options adjust.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.480
If the “Scale” value is higher than 1.00 (such as&nbsp;
in this example), this means you will need to size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.840
up your image to reach the higher resolution.&nbsp;
However, this might not always be necessary.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:33.200
Select the “Unit” option, then select cm/mm/in&nbsp;
and define the image width and height you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.040
In this example, we might only&nbsp;
want to print an image at 15x15cm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:45.120
The scale value adjusts back down to 1.77.&nbsp;
This means that we’re gonna have to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.880
double the image size we have now. If we&nbsp;
do this, our artwork might become blurry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.440
unless you have used vector layers for&nbsp;
your drawings. For more information on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.240
vector layers, go check out my previous tutorial!&nbsp;
https://tips.clip-studio.com/en-us/articles/3868

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.280
The “Interpolation method” option refers to&nbsp;
different ways your image can be scaled up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.920
or down. It makes sense to leave this&nbsp;
at “High accuracy” for most cases.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.080
We can now press “OK” and the image will be&nbsp;
adjusted according to your specifications.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.680
Next, if you want to add bleed to your artwork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.400
you can do this by simply going to “View”&nbsp;
&gt; “Crop Mark / Default border settings”.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.720
Now you can adjust the same values as&nbsp;
previously discussed in the tutorial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.040
Keep in mind that you won’t be able to extend&nbsp;
the canvas size in this window, and if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.480
canvas size is already at the size of the final&nbsp;
artwork you desire, you may have to first extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:49.360
the canvas size by going to “Edit” &gt; “Change&nbsp;
Canvas Size” and then come back to this window.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.160
Exporting full color images in CMYK

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.600
Clip Studio Paint is primarily&nbsp;
set up to work in RGB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.040
a color space that mixes&nbsp;
colors in an additive manner.&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:11.040
However, for print we will work in CMYK, a color&nbsp;
space that mixes colors in a subtractive manner.&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.840
If you’re unfamiliar with these terms,&nbsp;
I’d like to briefly explain them.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:23.840
First, “additive” colors. In RGB, which stands for&nbsp;
Red, Green and Blue, we increase each color value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:30.640
to brighten the overall color. If all three are&nbsp;
set to the maximum of 255, we have a white color.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:40.000
Now, “subtractive” colors. In CMYK, which stands&nbsp;
for Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Black (or Key Plate)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.320
we increase each color value&nbsp;
to darken the overall color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:50.080
If all three are set to the maximum of 100%,&nbsp;
we will have a very dark and saturated color.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.560
One important thing to note is that screens&nbsp;
can display brighter colors in a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:03.600
vivid way. In CMYK this usually is only possible&nbsp;
with spot colors, which we will address later.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.480
First, let’s see how we can work&nbsp;
with CMYK in Clip Studio Paint.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.920
Go to “View” and select the “Color&nbsp;
profile” &gt; “Preview settings” option.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.480
Before we move on, let’s quickly address the first&nbsp;
drop down option here, “Profile for preview”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.240
There are different color profiles in the world&nbsp;
of printing that pay respect to different papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:34.160
and colors. For example, if you create artwork&nbsp;
for a newspaper, you will need to use a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.080
color profile that limits the amount of&nbsp;
color that can be applied to the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.600
This is because newspaper soaks up color very&nbsp;
differently to the kind of paper you might use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:50.160
in an inkjet office printer. Things might end up&nbsp;
looking too dark if too much color is applied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.840
and color profiles try to&nbsp;
help optimize the end result.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.680
Make sure to look up the print specifications&nbsp;
on your printing service’s website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.560
to find out which color profile&nbsp;
you will need to work with!

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:09.440
But back to the window that just popped up.&nbsp;
Here, you can select your intended color profile.&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.280
Once you’ve done so, you’ll notice&nbsp;
that it looks a lot more dull now.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:21.280
We can however do a few things to improve this.&nbsp;
Let’s look at the “Rendering intent” drop-down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:28.000
menu. This offers a variety of options in which&nbsp;
your colors can be converted from RGB to CMYK!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.400
It can be a good idea to go for the "Saturation"&nbsp;
option in most cases. This option will often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.040
result in a much more similar look to&nbsp;
how your artwork was displayed in RGB.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.680
While you won’t be able to actually make the&nbsp;
colors more *saturated* (there is a limit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.600
to what standard CMYK colors can do), these&nbsp;
options DO change the contrast of your colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.640
And if you know your color theory, you know that&nbsp;
more contrast can make things look more vivid.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.720
If you are unhappy with all four “Rendering&nbsp;
intent” options, you can also select the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:08.240
“Tonal Correction” checkmark and fine tune the&nbsp;
contrast on all four color plates individually!

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.480
Once you are happy with the result, make sure to&nbsp;
select the “Save on canvas” option! Otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.840
your adjustments won’t actually be&nbsp;
saved when you export the image.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:23.040
You may want to now save your .csp file&nbsp;
as a new file with a different name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.400
to signify that this is the&nbsp;
CMYK version of your artwork.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:31.840
Otherwise, if you ever want to make a change,&nbsp;
you would have to redo the color adjustments!

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.040
Finally, let’s actually export the CMYK file.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.480
I would suggest exporting your file as a JPG&nbsp;
with the quality turned up all the way to 100,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.240
or as a TIFF file, which can offer&nbsp;
an even higher print quality.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.800
An export settings window&nbsp;
will pop up. The important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.360
thing here is to include the “Crop mark” option.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.000
Next, select “CMYK color” in&nbsp;
the “Expression color” menu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.720
Make sure to also “Embed ICC profile”.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:10.640
Finally, leave the “Scale ratio from&nbsp;
original data” at 100%. And that’s it!

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:17.760
This concludes the first part of the tutorial.&nbsp;
You now know how to export basic full color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.640
images for print in Clip Studio Paint. If&nbsp;
you’d like to know more about preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.840
an image such as this for a two tone spot color&nbsp;
print, stick around for more advanced techniques!

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.880
An Introduction to Spot Colors

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.640
I briefly teased the topic of spot colors earlier.&nbsp;
The thing is: Even in print you can achieve some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:46.560
very bright and vivid colors, such as this&nbsp;
neon green. This is possible with spot colors.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:55.440
Let’s remember how CMYK works. Colors are added&nbsp;
together. If you have a magnifying glass nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:01.120
why not try looking at a printed piece of paper&nbsp;
near you? Chances are, you will notice that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.920
picture is actually made up of tons of tiny&nbsp;
dots, that mix these four colors together!

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.480
Now, if something is printed in only one of these&nbsp;
base colors, it’s going to result in a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:18.000
smoother, cleaner look than if you were to mix&nbsp;
two of them together to create Green, for example.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:26.720
This is one reason why spot colors exist. Think&nbsp;
of them as pre-mixed colors. Using them will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:32.560
just result in a more even color application, but&nbsp;
it also enables brighter or more vivid colors.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.160
Clip Studio Paint sadly does not&nbsp;
yet have support for this technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.680
But, we can hack our way there. To figure out&nbsp;
how to do this, let’s first look at what a file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.240
looks like that’s been set up for print in&nbsp;
a program that does support spot colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.840
such as Photoshop, Illustrator or InDesign.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:58.240
Take this PDF for example. When opened in Adobe&nbsp;
Acrobat, I can access the “Print Production” menu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:03.280
and the “Output Preview” window from there.&nbsp;
Here, I am able to see that the neon green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:09.040
is listed as its own color alongside Cyan,&nbsp;
Magenta, Yellow and Black (or Key Plate).

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:14.160
If I deselect this color, I will now only see&nbsp;
what is being printed in the other colors (in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.840
case, only Black shows up). Likewise, I can turn&nbsp;
things around and see what happens if I deactivate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:24.400
all the CMYK color plates and only&nbsp;
have the spot color plate visible.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.840
As you can tell, the color disappears&nbsp;
and we only see Black left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.560
Because it basically is an instruction that&nbsp;
tells the printer “where you see Black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:37.520
print this other color and&nbsp;
where you don’t see Black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:44.160
don’t”. As you mouse over an area displayed&nbsp;
as Black, you will still see the percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:50.640
information next to the color plate reflect this&nbsp;
and display as “100%” for the spot color I picked.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:56.800
With that knowledge in mind, we are ready&nbsp;
to switch back over to Clip Studio Paint.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:01.840
Setting up Spot Colors in Clip Studio Paint

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:06.240
Before we start, let’s analyze&nbsp;
how I set up this artwork.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.080
You may have yours set up differently, but&nbsp;
I hope that I will still be able to show you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:16.320
the principles of setting up spot colors in a&nbsp;
way where you can apply it to your own artwork!

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:22.560
First, the actual artwork is on it’s own layer.&nbsp;
Everything that is not black is transparent.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:27.920
Next, the spot color is actually a&nbsp;
completely black layer where I used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:31.840
Clip Studio Paint’s “Layer Color”&nbsp;
effect to select the neon color.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.240
Inbetween the two, there is a layer with white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.360
color that I used to give the&nbsp;
illustration some highlights.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:46.320
To achieve something that looks like what we&nbsp;
saw in the PDF file before, we will now press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:53.200
CTRL and click the main artwork layer to create&nbsp;
a selection of it. Alternatively, you can right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:58.320
click the layer and select the “Selection&nbsp;
from Layer” &gt; “Create Selection” option.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:03.040
Next, we need to also select the white area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:09.360
To add it to the selection, hold down CTRL&nbsp;
+ SHIFT and click the layer. Alternatively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:14.960
you can right click the layer and select the&nbsp;
“Selection from Layer” &gt; “Add Selection” option.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:22.320
We now have everything selected that ISN’T&nbsp;
going to print in neon green. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:28.080
our next step is inverting that selection.&nbsp;
You can do so by pressing CTRL + SHIFT + I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:35.360
or going to “Select” &gt; “Invert Selected Area”.&nbsp;
Now, we actually have a selection of everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:41.760
that IS supposed to print in neon green, and we&nbsp;
can select that layer and create a new layer mask.

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:48.240
Next, let’s turn off the “Layer Color”&nbsp;
effect so the layer appears as fully black.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.840
What we see now is identical to&nbsp;
what we saw in the PDF file earlier.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:16:00.000
The next step is manually adding information&nbsp;
about which color this is supposed to print as.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:06.000
I’ve mentioned before that Clip Studio Paint&nbsp;
doesn’t (yet!) have any support for spot colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:12.560
such as PANTONE. However, most printers that are&nbsp;
able to print spot colors will still accept files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:17.520
that don’t have them baked in naturally and&nbsp;
instead are showing this information as text.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:24.000
This is why we’re going to use the Text tool&nbsp;
to simply write the color we want to print in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:28.320
outside of the main printing area and&nbsp;
in fact, even outside of the Bleed area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:32.720
(we don’t want to risk having this text&nbsp;
ever show up anywhere on a print!).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:38.400
The way I would suggest phrasing&nbsp;
it is “Print in [your color]”.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:42.480
To figure out which color you&nbsp;
want to print something in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:47.600
I would suggest investing in a PANTONE color&nbsp;
sampler. But if that is outside your budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:52.560
you can use their website to have a color&nbsp;
suggested to you. To do so, double click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:59.520
your currently active color. Note down the RGB&nbsp;
values, or alternatively, copy paste the Hex code.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:06.080
Next, go to www.pantone.com and select&nbsp;
the “Find a Pantone Color” menu option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:12.720
On the left, select “Convert” &gt;&nbsp;
“RGB/CMYK/Hex”. In the “Color Space” section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.320
select either RGB or Hex,&nbsp;
and input your information.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:23.440
Before you press “Search”, you need to select&nbsp;
the correct Color Library. If you are unsure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:28.720
which one to use, you can always select&nbsp;
“All Pantone Books”. For this example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:34.480
I already know I want a neon color, so I will&nbsp;
only select “PANTONE Pastels &amp; Neons Coated”.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:40.000
A quick explanation of “Coated”&nbsp;
and “Uncoated” color profiles:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:47.520
These refer to the paper quality. Anytime a paper&nbsp;
has a smooth or glossy texture, you’re probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:54.000
looking at a coated paper. This usually is the&nbsp;
case for most photo or magazine prints. Anytime a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:59.840
paper has a coarse and natural feeling texture,&nbsp;
you’re probably looking at an uncoated paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:06.800
This is usually the case for newspapers or eco&nbsp;
friendly packaging. As mentioned before, different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:14.240
paper types soak up color differently, which&nbsp;
is why PANTONE offers separate color profiles.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:18.400
Once I’ve pressed “Search”, I will get&nbsp;
a suggestion from PANTONE’s website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.840
for what color to use. Your screen isn’t going to&nbsp;
accurately reflect how this will look on paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.120
If you can’t afford the samples, contact&nbsp;
your printer and ask them for a suggestion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:33.680
Maybe they also have samples they can&nbsp;
send or show you to help you decide.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:41.840
But now, let’s go back into our file, and simply&nbsp;
add this PANTONE color into the text information!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.880
You can also do this for the Black color&nbsp;
plate to be extra clear in your communication.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.800
Exporting Spot Color images

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.480
Now, when we export this file we&nbsp;
have to consider a few factors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:19:00.240
Previously, we’ve exported a CMYK file that was&nbsp;
a full color image. However, in this example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:05.280
we only want to print in two color plates&nbsp;
- Black and the PANTONE spot color.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:12.720
When we export our files this time, we are going&nbsp;
to export two separate TIFF files - one for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:18.400
spot color plate and one for the black color&nbsp;
plate. Before exporting, make sure that both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:24.080
the spot color and the black color plate are&nbsp;
displayed as a pure RGB Black (R=0 G=0 B=0), and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:28.560
then in the Export Settings window,&nbsp;
select “Gray” as the Expression Color.

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:34.960
When splitting up the color plates like this,&nbsp;
it would also be helpful to send your printer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:40.720
a preview of how the final artwork is supposed&nbsp;
to look. I would recommend exporting a RGB JPG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:46.400
file of your artwork and sending it off to your&nbsp;
printer alongside the TIFF files we just created.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:52.560
Even though we added crop marks and print color&nbsp;
information onto the image itself already, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:56.800
can also be helpful to add the final print&nbsp;
size (after cropping) into the file name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:00.000
as well as which color plate each&nbsp;
file is supposed to represent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:05.840
It’s never a bad thing to have file naming&nbsp;
conventions that communicate clearly.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.720
And while we’re talking about communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:13.760
most important of all: Always make sure to check&nbsp;
that your printer understands what you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:20.080
and will tell you if there are any changes you&nbsp;
might have to make for an optimal printing result!

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:20.720
And that’s it!

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.800
Before ending this tutorial, I have two&nbsp;
short notes I quickly want to address!

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:28.560
Overprint

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:35.040
Keep in mind that you have the ability to&nbsp;
overprint colors. This means that you can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:41.360
for example, print 100% of your spot color in the&nbsp;
same space as where you are also printing 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:44.800
of black. It’s useful to keep this option in mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:47.840
especially when you want to&nbsp;
print something like this:

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:53.280
Unlike our previous example, this image&nbsp;
has shading in various tones rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:58.800
cell shading with clear edges. To have the&nbsp;
shading applied to the green spot color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:04.160
we need to print the spot color even in areas&nbsp;
where we also print black. Keep in mind that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:08.400
those areas are going to be darker than&nbsp;
those where you are ONLY printing in black.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:14.720
Let’s go back to our previous example to see&nbsp;
when you might NOT want to include overprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:20.000
options. If I were to not exclude the black&nbsp;
artwork itself and just exported the spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:24.400
color plate with the white highlights, the&nbsp;
final print would look something like this:

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.560
The big circles surrounding the&nbsp;
white sections don’t exactly look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:33.920
flattering. So make sure to consider what kind of&nbsp;
look you want when setting up the spot color area.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:37.920
Special Print Effects

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:44.960
I also want to bring to your attention&nbsp;
that you can use this technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:49.249
to set up files for special printing&nbsp;
techniques, such as UV coating (parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.249 --> 00:21:51.920
of the image will reflect light or appear shiny),&nbsp;
glitter coating (sparkly!) or many other effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:57.840
Contact your printer about what options they have&nbsp;
on offer, and then simply set up the file as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:04.560
would a spot color plate file, except describing&nbsp;
that file as “Print as [desired effect]”.

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:11.840
Thank you for reading! If you have any questions,&nbsp;
feel free to leave them in the comments!

